{
  "heading": "Outgoing HTTP: Tac",
  "axis": "language",
  "summary": "In the browser, HTTPClient is the auto-managed transport. HTTPClient takes only cache: no-store by default, or cache-first, network-first or reload. A Tac HTTPClient must declare at least one request method, such as GetRequest. Like a Spring @HttpExchange interface, a client has no implementation: JavaScript and TypeScript write a class whose methods have empty bodies, and a service injects it with Needs. Swift, Kotlin, C#, F#, Rust, Go and Dart write the client in their bodiless form (a protocol, an interface, an F# type with only abstract members, a trait, a Go interface or an abstract interface class) in client/clients. Tachyon generates the client and its behavior context from the Setup with the same stem (CatalogClient uses CatalogSetup), whose HTTP adapter sends the request. The service builds the client: CatalogClient() in Swift, Kotlin, F# and Dart, new CatalogClient() in C#, CatalogClient::new() in Rust and NewCatalogClient() in Go. A client with a method body, a field or another role, such as a service that is also an HTTPClient, fails with TY1308. A request to another origin needs its host in `tac.outgoing.hosts`: the build checks request URLs, and the page's `connect-src` lets the browser reach only these hosts.",
